    "guide": [
      [
        "Noise cancelling is not all equal",
        "<p>Claims such as 44 dB or 50 dB describe best-case lab conditions, so compare reviewer verdicts instead. Flagship models from Sony and Bose are described as stronger than budget ANC, while cheap ANC may cut low rumble but let voices through. Transparency mode matters for safety when walking.</p>"
      ],
      [
        "Match the codec and phone",
        "<p>iPhones use AAC and gain most from AirPods features. Android phones can use LDAC on Sony and Soundcore models. Multipoint lets you connect two devices at once, which is useful for a laptop and phone, and is listed for several models here.</p>"
      ],
      [
        "Battery, water rating and fit",
        "<p>Quoted battery usually refers to ANC off, so expect less in real use. IPX4 handles sweat and splashes, IP55 adds dust resistance, and IP68 is the sturdiest rating here. Fit matters more than specs, so try silicone tips and check the return policy.</p>"
      ],
      [
        "Warranty and buying channel",
        "<p>ProductNation advises buying from official sellers to get a proper warranty, because grey-market earbuds may not be covered locally. Promotions on Shopee and Lazada sales can cut 20 percent or more, so watch prices for a few weeks, and keep your invoice for claims.</p>"
      ],
      [
        "Calls, microphones and wind noise",
        "<p>Spec sheets rarely say how earbuds sound on a call. ProductNation and others comment on call quality for some models, such as the Jabra Elite 10, but outdoors wind can still overwhelm any microphone. If you take many calls, favour models with several microphones and read reviews that mention voice pickup.</p>"
      ],
      [
        "Fit, comfort and long wear",
        "<p>Earbuds that feel fine for ten minutes can ache after two hours. Ear tips come in several sizes, and a poor seal weakens both bass and noise cancelling, so test them in the shop or buy where returns are easy. Smaller buds like the OPPO Enco Buds2 suit small ears.</p>"
      ],
      [
        "Companion apps, EQ and firmware",
        "<p>Apps such as Sony Sound Connect let you adjust equaliser settings, noise cancelling levels and controls. Firmware updates can improve performance after launch, so choose brands that keep supporting their earbuds, and check that the app is available for your phone.</p>"
      ],
      [
        "Battery ageing and replacement",
        "<p>Tiny batteries wear out within a few years of daily use, and most earbuds cannot be repaired cheaply. That is a strong argument against overpaying for flagship models if you replace phones and earbuds often. Treat quoted battery hours as the best case for a new pair.</p>"
      ]
    ],
    "faq": [
      [
        "Are expensive earbuds worth it?",
        "They tend to have stronger noise cancelling, better call microphones and longer support. For quiet use, cheaper models such as the OPPO Enco Buds2 are enough."
      ],
      [
        "Which earbuds suit iPhone and which suit Android?",
        "AirPods Pro suit iPhones best. Samsung, Sony, OnePlus and Soundcore models suit Android, with LDAC available on some."
      ],
      [
        "Do cheap earbuds have a warranty in Malaysia?",
        "Many do not state one clearly. Buy from official stores and ask for the warranty term in writing before paying."
      ],
      [
        "What is a good budget for earbuds?",
        "Between RM250 and RM500 buys good noise cancelling from Soundcore, OnePlus or Sony. Under RM150 you mostly give up ANC."
      ]
    ]
