    "guide": [
      [
        "How much memory and storage do students need?",
        "<p>Sixteen gigabytes of memory and 512 gigabytes of storage is a comfortable baseline for several years of study. Eight gigabytes can work for basic tasks but may slow down with many browser tabs. Design and engineering courses may need more.</p>"
      ],
      [
        "Windows, macOS and Arm laptops",
        "<p>Check your faculty's software list before choosing. Some engineering and accounting tools run only on Windows, and Arm-based Windows laptops such as the Snapdragon X Vivobook may not run every app. MacBooks suit creative work but cost more.</p>"
      ],
      [
        "Weight, battery and portability",
        "<p>If you carry your laptop daily, weight matters. The HP Pavilion Aero 13 at 0.99 kilograms and the MacBook Air at 1.24 kilograms are light, while the Vivobook 16 at 1.88 kilograms is not. Claimed battery life is usually higher than real use.</p>"
      ],
      [
        "Warranty, education discounts and where to buy",
        "<p>Warranty details were rarely stated in our sources, so confirm terms with the seller. Apple and others offer education pricing at certain times, and Shopee and Lazada official stores often run sales. Keep your invoice for any claim.</p>"
      ],
      [
        "Spec traps to avoid",
        "<p>Entry models may ship with 8 GB of memory or slower storage, and the same model name can cover different configurations. Always check the exact processor, memory and storage on the product page before paying.</p>"
      ],
      [
        "Screen, keyboard and webcam",
        "<p>You will stare at the screen for hours, so check the resolution and brightness. A comfortable keyboard matters for essays, and a decent webcam and microphone matter for online classes. OLED panels, such as the Acer's, look better but can cost more.</p>"
      ],
      [
        "Ports, charging and accessories",
        "<p>Check for a USB-C port, HDMI for projectors and a headphone jack. Thin laptops may have few ports, so budget for a hub, a mouse and a sleeve. A compact charger is easier to carry to campus.</p>"
      ],
      [
        "Should you buy new, older or refurbished?",
        "<p>Older models can offer better value, as the MacBook Air M4 shows against the M5. Refurbished laptops are cheaper, but warranty terms vary, so buy from sellers that state their return policy and warranty in writing.</p>"
      ],
      [
        "Software and security basics",
        "<p>Many universities give students free office software and antivirus licences, so check before paying for them. Set up automatic updates, back up coursework to cloud storage and a drive, and register the warranty as soon as the laptop arrives.</p>"
      ]
    ],
    "faq": [
      [
        "How much should I spend on a student laptop in Malaysia?",
        "Between about RM2,100 and RM3,500 covers most coursework. Spending more buys weight savings, screens or an Apple chip."
      ],
      [
        "Is a MacBook worth it for students?",
        "It can be if you want a light, durable laptop and your course software supports macOS. It costs more than Windows alternatives."
      ],
      [
        "Do I need 16 GB of memory?",
        "It is a comfortable baseline for several years, although 8 GB can handle basic tasks."
      ],
      [
        "Which laptop is lightest?",
        "Among the models here, the HP Pavilion Aero 13 at 0.99 kilograms is the lightest in our sources."
      ]
    ]
